# KNOWN GAP — `gg check` ACCEPTS, then the C compiler FAILS on the emitted code.
#
# A comprehension whose TARGET PATTERN is a tuple, over a Dict source:
#
#     [mk(val, "!") for (k, val) in dsrc]
#
# `gg check` reports rc 0 ("no semantic errors"), then `gg build` exits 1 with a
# raw C diagnostic leaking to the user:
#
#     error: incompatible types when assigning to type 'Str' from type 'i...'
#
# The STATEMENT form of the identical iteration is completely correct:
#
#     for (k, val) in dsrc:
#         v.push(mk(val, "!"))
#
# so the discriminator is COMPREHENSION-vs-STATEMENT at the same target pattern,
# not the tuple destructuring itself and not the Dict source. `for (k, v) in d`
# is documented idiomatic (docs/book/05-collections.md).
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): this compiles and runs, printing the same thing the
# statement form prints. The tuple target is a supported pattern, so the fix is
# to LOWER it in the comprehension arm -- not to reject it. Core #10
# (lower-or-reject: no arm may accept what it cannot lower) and Core #8 (an
# accepted program that fails at codegen is a defect, never a parity excuse).
#
# LANE NOTE (succession): the SELF-HOST lane REJECTS this program rather than
# emitting broken C, so on this shape the self-host is MORE correct than Rust gg
# -- oracle hygiene says fix the Rust side; do not dumb the self-host down.
# Measured 2026-08-24 by R44 Track-K brief-review pass 4, orchestrator-verified.

from std.collections import Dict

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Dict[String, String] dsrc = {}
    dsrc.put("alpha", "one")
    auto v = [mk(val, "!") for (k, val) in dsrc]
    print(v.len())
    print(v.get(0).unwrap())
